<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leon Battista Alberti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a>–<a href="/years/1472">1472</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leon Battista Alberti was an Italian humanist, architect and writer, one of the founding figures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art theory. His treatises On Painting and On the Art of Building set out the principles of perspective and of classical architecture. His buildings include the façade of Santa Maria Novella in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a> in Rimini and Sant'Andrea in Mantua.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti.jpg" alt="Leon Battista Alberti" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leon Battista Alberti · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leon_Battista_Alberti,_Self-Portrait,_c._1435,_NGA_43845.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leon Battista Alberti was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> on 14 February 1404. His father, Lorenzo di Benedetto Alberti, was a wealthy Florentine who had been exiled from his own city and was allowed to return in 1428. Alberti was sent to boarding school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. In 1431 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, took holy orders and entered the service of the papal court, where his study of ancient ruins stirred his interest in architecture.</p><p>By the age of twenty he had written a comedy, Philodoxius, that was passed off as a genuine classical work and later deceived the editor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius">Aldus Manutius</a>. He never married.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1435 Alberti began Della pittura, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a> in its Italian version, which analysed perspective, composition and colour and has been called the first scientific study of perspective. He held that mathematics underlay the arts and defined beauty as the harmony of all parts in relation to one another. His De re aedificatoria, completed in 1452 on the basis of Vitruvius, was the first architectural treatise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, covering subjects from history and town planning to engineering and aesthetics. It was dedicated to Pope Nicholas V and not printed until 1485.</p><p>He wrote widely beyond the arts. I libri della famiglia, in the Tuscan dialect, discussed education, marriage and household management; Momus was a comedy about the gods; and he composed the first Italian grammar and a work on ciphers. He is credited with inventing the first polyalphabetic cipher, and David Kahn called him the father of Western cryptography.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1438 Alberti turned more to architecture, encouraged by Leonello d'Este of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. In 1446 he designed the façade of the Rucellai Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, executed by Bernardo Rossellino, and in 1447 he became architectural adviser to Nicholas V. Sigismondo Malatesta commissioned him in 1450 to transform the Gothic church of San Francesco in Rimini into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a>, whose façade was left incomplete.</p><p>At Santa Maria Novella in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> he designed the upper façade, bridging nave and aisles with two large scrolls that became a standard feature of later church façades. He also designed two churches in Mantua, San Sebastiano, which was never completed, and Sant'Andrea, which was finished after his death and is considered his most significant work. He interested himself in the look and design of buildings rather than their construction. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 25 April 1472, aged 68.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leon Battista Alberti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…chancellors Salutati and Bruni; Poggio Bracciolini, the finder of manuscripts; Niccolò Niccoli, who spent his fortune on books; Lorenzo Valla, the sharpest critic of texts of his age; <b>Leon Battista Alberti</b>, who wrote on painting, architecture and the family; Ficino, the translator of Plato; and Pico della Mirandola.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta, a brilliant and ruthless captain, had <b>Leon Battista Alberti</b> remodel the church of San Francesco as the Tempio Malatestiano.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-rimini">Lordship of Rimini</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1448" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leon Battista Alberti"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 3760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5544 3760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1448">What was here in 1448? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
